1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,787
Hello everyone, and welcome back. We
are QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

2
00:00:04,787 --> 00:00:09,056
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

3
00:00:09,433 --> 00:00:14,212
<font color="#7FD4E6">And today, we meet three codes that
can fix any error on a single qubit.</font>

4
00:00:16,733 --> 00:00:20,799
Last time we ended on a question,
whether a code can catch every possible

5
00:00:20,799 --> 00:00:25,090
error on a single qubit, and the answer
is yes, as long as the lightest error

6
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:28,704
that slips past all of its checks has a
weight of at least three.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:32,393
Peter Shor found the first
code like that in 1995, and

8
00:00:32,393 --> 00:00:35,252
it spends nine physical
qubits to protect a single

9
00:00:35,252 --> 00:00:38,461
qubit of information, which
does feel like rather a lot.

10
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:41,909
So here's something for you to
guess before we start. What do

11
00:00:41,909 --> 00:00:44,832
you think is the fewest qubits
that could possibly do the job?

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,899
<font color="#7FD4E6">Hmm, there are three kinds
of error, and the bit flip</font>

13
00:00:49,899 --> 00:00:53,723
<font color="#7FD4E6">code needed three qubits
for just one kind, so nine?</font>

14
00:00:54,067 --> 00:00:57,722
Well, that's a very natural way to
count, and it's the way Shor's code

15
00:00:57,722 --> 00:01:01,112
is put together. But the true
answer is smaller than nine, and by

16
00:01:01,113 --> 00:01:04,609
the end you'll be able to see why,
just by counting something else.

17
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:09,096
Let's write Shor's code the
way we learned to in the last

18
00:01:09,096 --> 00:01:12,006
two sections, as a list of
checks, which for nine qubits

19
00:01:12,006 --> 00:01:14,916
sitting in three blocks of
three comes to eight strings.

20
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,467
The first six are Z pairs
inside each block, just like

21
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:21,378
the checks of the bit flip
code, so each block on its

22
00:01:21,378 --> 00:01:24,233
own can catch a bit flip on
any of its three qubits.

23
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:29,104
The last two put an X on six qubits at a
time, and they compare the signs of two

24
00:01:29,104 --> 00:01:33,625
neighbouring blocks, which is how a phase
flip anywhere inside a block gets noticed.

25
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,399
If you're wondering how a check like that
actually gets measured, here is the

26
00:01:38,399 --> 00:01:42,654
circuit for the first one, where an extra
ancilla qubit starts in zero and two

27
00:01:42,654 --> 00:01:46,909
CNOTs copy the parity of qubits one and
two onto it, just as in lecture seven.

28
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:51,941
An X check is read the other way round,
with a Hadamard on the ancilla before

29
00:01:51,941 --> 00:01:55,973
and after, and in between the ancilla
controls an X on each of its six

30
00:01:55,973 --> 00:02:00,122
qubits, so the ancilla reads one only
when that check answers minus one.

31
00:02:00,567 --> 00:02:04,743
<font color="#7FD4E6">But the X checks and the Z checks
overlap. Do they still commute?</font>

32
00:02:05,067 --> 00:02:09,214
They do. Every Z pair sits either
completely inside an X check or

33
00:02:09,214 --> 00:02:13,425
completely outside it, so the two
share either two qubits or none,

34
00:02:13,425 --> 00:02:16,535
and an even number of clashes
means they commute.

35
00:02:17,133 --> 00:02:20,793
And with nine qubits and eight
independent checks, each one cutting

36
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:25,064
the space in half, we're left with two
dimensions, which is one logical qubit.

37
00:02:25,633 --> 00:02:29,807
Now here is something I found strange
the first time I saw it, which is that

38
00:02:29,807 --> 00:02:34,093
the operator acting as a logical X on
this code is made entirely of Z letters.

39
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:38,664
<font color="#7FD4E6">Wait, so Z on all nine qubits
flips the logical bit?</font>

40
00:02:39,733 --> 00:02:44,935
Yes. Z on the three qubits of a block
turns the plus inside that block into a

41
00:02:44,935 --> 00:02:50,205
minus, and logical zero and logical one
differ by just those three signs, so Z

42
00:02:50,205 --> 00:02:55,201
on all nine swaps them, while X on all
nine ends up playing the logical Z.

43
00:02:56,733 --> 00:02:59,864
Andrew Steane found a code that
does the same job with seven

44
00:02:59,864 --> 00:03:03,048
qubits, and it comes from a
classical idea, which is to write

45
00:03:03,049 --> 00:03:06,233
the numbers one to seven in
binary as the columns of a table.

46
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:10,085
Each row of this table picks
out four of the qubits, so if

47
00:03:10,085 --> 00:03:13,451
you draw each row as a circle
around its four, you get three

48
00:03:13,451 --> 00:03:16,303
overlapping circles with one
qubit in every region.

49
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:21,765
Every circle carries two checks, X on its
four qubits and Z on the same four, so

50
00:03:21,765 --> 00:03:26,446
that's six checks on seven qubits, which
once again leaves one logical qubit.

51
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:31,787
And any two circles share two qubits,
while a circle's own X and Z checks

52
00:03:31,787 --> 00:03:36,211
share all four, so every overlap is
even and all six checks commute.

53
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:40,013
So now suppose an X error hits
qubit five. Before I light

54
00:03:40,013 --> 00:03:43,570
anything up, which circles do you
think are going to notice it?

55
00:03:44,767 --> 00:03:49,302
<font color="#7FD4E6">Five sits inside circles one and
three, but it's outside circle two.</font>

56
00:03:49,633 --> 00:03:53,687
Exactly. So the three Z checks
answer one, zero, one, and if

57
00:03:53,687 --> 00:03:57,603
you read that as a number in
binary, it comes out as five.

58
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:01,659
You can watch the same thing
happen in the circuit, where each

59
00:04:01,659 --> 00:04:05,288
circle gets its own ancilla, and
the X on qubit five only reaches

60
00:04:05,288 --> 00:04:08,463
the two ancillas that have a CNOT
coming from qubit five.

61
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:12,064
<font color="#7FD4E6">Oh, so the syndrome is just
the address of the qubit?</font>

62
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,069
That's it. Each qubit's column in
the table is its own number

63
00:04:16,069 --> 00:04:19,982
written in binary, so whichever
qubit is hit, the syndrome spells

64
00:04:19,982 --> 00:04:23,956
out where, and a Z error does the
same thing through the X checks.

65
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,125
The smallest code of all uses five
qubits, and its four checks are

66
00:04:30,125 --> 00:04:34,608
really one string, X Z Z X I,
shifted one place along each time.

67
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,591
Even a mixed check like this one
can be measured the same way,

68
00:04:38,591 --> 00:04:42,149
with the ancilla sitting between
two Hadamards and controlling an

69
00:04:42,148 --> 00:04:45,706
X on the first and fourth qubits
and a Z on the second and third.

70
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:49,694
And now we can finally answer the
question from the start, where the trick

71
00:04:49,694 --> 00:04:52,949
is just to count the different cases
that a syndrome has to tell apart.

72
00:04:53,533 --> 00:04:58,650
On n qubits, each qubit can suffer an X,
a Y or a Z, which makes three n single-

73
00:04:58,650 --> 00:05:03,637
qubit errors, and then there is one more
case where nothing went wrong at all.

74
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:08,518
With one logical qubit you have n
minus one checks, and they give two to

75
00:05:08,518 --> 00:05:12,864
the n minus one different syndromes,
so if every case needs a syndrome of

76
00:05:12,864 --> 00:05:16,666
its own, three n plus one can be at
most two to the n minus one.

77
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:22,478
<font color="#7FD4E6">So let me try. Three qubits gives
ten cases, but only four syndromes.</font>

78
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:27,351
Right. Four qubits gives thirteen
cases and still only eight syndromes,

79
00:05:27,351 --> 00:05:31,837
and five gives sixteen cases and
exactly sixteen syndromes, so five is

80
00:05:31,837 --> 00:05:35,868
the first size that fits, with not a
single syndrome left over.

81
00:05:36,967 --> 00:05:39,939
<font color="#7FD4E6">So my nine was almost
twice what we need.</font>

82
00:05:40,267 --> 00:05:44,657
It was, but it was a very reasonable
nine, since Shor's code was the first

83
00:05:44,657 --> 00:05:48,806
one anyone found. And I should be
honest that this count assumes every

84
00:05:48,806 --> 00:05:52,835
error needs its own syndrome, which
Shor's code doesn't bother with,

85
00:05:52,835 --> 00:05:57,285
because a Z on qubit one and a Z on
qubit two look identical to its checks.

86
00:05:57,867 --> 00:06:01,648
That turns out to be harmless, since
the two of them multiply to one of

87
00:06:01,648 --> 00:06:05,321
the checks, and fixing the wrong one
does nothing at all. A different

88
00:06:05,322 --> 00:06:08,995
argument shows that even codes like
that can't get below five qubits.

89
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:13,363
So a code that fixes any
single-qubit error needs a

90
00:06:13,363 --> 00:06:16,476
different syndrome for every
way one qubit can go wrong,

91
00:06:16,477 --> 00:06:19,647
and five qubits turn out to
be just enough room for that.

92
00:06:20,067 --> 00:06:25,334
<font color="#7FD4E6">Shor's and Steane's checks were all X's or
all Z's, though. Is that a coincidence?</font>

93
00:06:25,667 --> 00:06:30,264
Not a coincidence at all. In section eight
point four we'll see how codes like those

94
00:06:30,264 --> 00:06:34,417
two can be built from a pair of classical
codes, one for each kind of error.
